Reviewed pages: https://www.adavitrust.org; https://www.adavitrust.org/copy-of-education-outreach; https://www.adavitrust.org/team-4; https://www.adavitrust.org/contact-8', 'approved', now(), 'admin', false, 'Adavi Trust Adavi Trust works for sustainable forest-based livelihoods and conservation through collaboration with indigenous communities. About | Adavi Trust top of page About Themes All projects Meet the Team News & Publications Adavi Dialogues Get in touch More Use tab to navigate through the menu items. About Us We believe that everyone can be empowered to champion the wild. Adavi literally translates to `forest'' or `wilderness'' in three South Indian languages. At Adavi we are determined to aid the conservation of India''s fast disappearing natural landscapes. We create practical grass-root based interventions grounded in deep ecological philosophy to promote wildlife conservation, education, and sustainability. Adavi believes that conservation should be led by local communities and guided by science if and when required. A balanced, fair and just approach to conserving our wildlife is the need of the hour and something we strive towards. At Adavi we practice a hands-on approach on a daily basis in an off-grid field station surrounded by forests, fields, and savannah grasslands. Our Field Station The Adavi field station is a vibrant oasis situated in the Rayalaseema region of Andhra Pradesh. We are humbled to be part of an ecosystem built over millennia in the world''s oldest volcanic plateau - the Deccan plateau. South India India Forest Conservation Livelihoods Indigenous Rights South India Grassroot/Implementation CSOs CRA Partner Map workbook Forest Conservation Livelihoods Indigenous Rights South India Grassroot/Implementation CSOs Adavi Trust Forest Conservation Livelihoods Indigenous Rights Youth Farmers Children Entrepreneurs Communities Rural communities South India Society About | Adavi Trust top of page About Themes All projects Meet the Team News & Publications Adavi Dialogues Get in touch More Use tab to navigate through the menu items.', now()),
('cso-co-existence-consortium-08a360eb', 'Co-existence Consortium', 'A multi-institutional collective promoting research, communication, and policy for coexistence between people and wildlife.', 'Coexistence | Coexistence Consortium top of page About Us Members Coexistence Resources by Our Members Fellowship Coexistence Fellows 2022 Cohort Coexistence Fellows 2023 Cohort Fellowship Bulletin Champions Coexistence Champions 2025 Cohort Outreach Webinars Blog Newsletters BLR Exhibition Connect More Use tab to navigate through the menu items. Coexistence is an attempt to rethink the dominant conservation paradigm - not just protecting wild animals locked away in far-off pockets of forests, but also coexisting and living well with animals and nature around us. We are a consortium of ecologists, anthropologists, geographers and conservationists with extensive knowledge and experience in human-wildlife coexistence who have come together to promote the concept of coexistence. The focal activity of this consortium is to gain deeper insights into coexistence between humans and nature by supporting a range of context-specific coexistence projects around the world. The initial funding to set up the Coexistence Consortium came from the Lantana Elephants Exhibitions happening around the world, titled "Coexistence" and celebrating the human-nature connection. Latest Updates Coexistence in', 'Pan-India, India', null, null, null, 'India', 'email@example.com', '2545352419', 'https://www.coexistenceconsortium.com', '{"instagram": "https://www.instagram.com/coexistenceconsortium", "facebook": "https://www.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100090640694372", "twitter": "https://twitter.com/CoexConsortium", "youtube": "https://www.youtube.com/@coexistenceconsortium", "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/company/coexistenceconsortium"}'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, ARRAY['Human-Wildlife Coexistence', 'Research', 'Advocacy', 'Pan-India', 'India', 'Networks/Alliances', 'CRA Partner Map workbook']::text[]